                    round_name_r4:          `Recover`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Map the collision before you move anything`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `It is 7:40 a.m. Room 300 — your <strong>only hybrid room that seats 50</strong> — is flooded and out for 9 days. Two faculty just changed their availability and the calendar is imploding. Before you touch a single booking, build the picture: every hard constraint, the full dependency chain, and the <strong>single binding constraint</strong> everything else must bend around. The classic operations error is to <em>fix one conflict and create three downstream</em> — so trace the cascade first. Cohort A's Day 1 is 48 hours away and contractually needs that hybrid room; its lead faculty is now Wednesday-morning only.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Re-sequence faculty & facilities`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Build a feasible week. Where does Cohort A run its hybrid Day 1 — the <strong>hotel suite</strong> ($16,800, 42% of the budget), <strong>split rooms</strong>, or <strong>virtual</strong>? Re-time Cohort A to its faculty's <strong>Wednesday-morning</strong> window without breaking the learning sequence — and remember Cohort B was <em>promised Room 300</em> for its marquee Thursday guest session. Every move has a consequence: split rooms need a second coordinator; virtual changes the experience and the catering; and bumping Cohort B protects one renewal while damaging an international relationship.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Allocate scarce staff & the budget`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Assign 3 senior + 4 junior coordinators across both cohorts and lock the spend against the <strong>$40,000</strong> cap. The tensions are explicit: <strong>Maria</strong>, the senior both cohorts want, is already at overtime risk — assigning her to both costs 1.5× and risks a fatigue-driven service drop on your highest-stakes client. The hotel suite protects Cohort A but eats the budget. Juniors cost less but need supervision. The discipline is to protect a <strong>≥ 8.5 on the portfolio</strong>, not to rob Cohort B to save Cohort A.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover & stabilize`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Tuesday afternoon. The MeridianHealth CHRO emails: <em>"Is Day 1 fully handled?"</em> — and a junior coordinator just called in sick. Silence reads as chaos; a clean, confident note 24 hours early reads as control. Absorb the staff loss without breaching overtime or budget, communicate proactively to both clients, and put a <strong>monitoring routine</strong> in place — who watches what, who decides — so the <em>second surprise</em> doesn't sink the week. Operations is the business of the second surprise.`,

                    r1_binding_label:       `What is the single binding constraint every other decision must bend around?`,
                    binding_room:           `Room 300 out of service — the only hybrid room that seats 50, gone for 9 days`,
                    binding_faculty:        `The faculty availability changes — re-time everything around the teachers first`,
                    binding_budget:         `The $40k budget — minimize spend and let the rest fall into place`,
                    binding_staff:          `The coordinator shortage — staffing is what will sink the week`,
                    r1_dependency_label:    `How do you handle the dependency cascade?`,
                    dep_mapfirst:           `Map the full cascade first — trace what each move breaks downstream before committing one`,
                    dep_loudest:            `Fix the loudest problem now — get Cohort A a room, then deal with whatever breaks`,
                    dep_parallel:           `Let each role fix its own piece in parallel and reconcile at the end`,
                    r1_awareness_label:     `How fully has your team registered the new faculty windows (Cohort A lead = Wed morning only; Cohort B guest = Thu fixed)? (%)`,
                    r1_awareness_readout:   `Constraint awareness before re-sequencing:`,

                    r2_facility_label:      `Where does Cohort A run its hybrid Day 1?`,
                    fac_suite:              `Rent the hotel conference suite — full hybrid, $4,200/day × 4 = $16,800 (42% of budget)`,
                    fac_split:              `Split into two smaller campus rooms — needs a second coordinator, minor experience cost (~$1,800)`,
                    fac_virtual:            `Push Day 1 mostly virtual — cheap (~$600), but changes the experience and the catering`,
                    fac_keep300:            `Keep Cohort A in Room 300 and hope the dry-out finishes early ($0)`,
                    r2_timing_label:        `Re-time Cohort A to its lead faculty's new window:`,
                    tim_wedam:              `Wednesday morning start — matches the faculty's only window, keeps the learning sequence`,
                    tim_wedpm:              `Keep the Wednesday afternoon slot as planned on the master calendar`,
                    tim_thu:                `Slide Cohort A to Thursday to buy a day — break the Day-1 sequence`,
                    r2_cohortb_label:       `What happens to Cohort B's promised Thursday room for its guest faculty?`,
                    cob_protect:            `Protect it — keep Cohort B in a proper room for the marquee Thursday session`,
                    cob_downgrade:          `Downgrade Cohort B to a smaller room — give the better space to Cohort A`,
                    cob_bump:               `Bump Cohort B's guest session — Cohort A's renewal comes first`,

                    r3_maria_label:         `Maria, the senior coordinator both cohorts want, is already at overtime risk. What do you do with her?`,
                    mar_protect:            `Protect her — one cohort only, backfill the rest with a junior + a named backup`,
                    mar_onecohort:          `Lead cohort only — give her Cohort A and pair her with a junior`,
                    mar_both:               `Assign her to BOTH flagship cohorts — she's the best, at 1.5× overtime cost`,
                    r3_balance_label:       `How do you split your 3 senior coordinators across the two cohorts?`,
                    bal_portfolio:          `Portfolio balance — senior cover on both, juniors backstopped on each`,
                    bal_aheavy:             `Load Cohort A — two seniors + the good room on A, a lone junior on B`,
                    bal_thin:               `Spread one senior across both and lean on juniors to save hours`,
                    r3_catering_label:      `Catering & materials spend for both cohorts this week ($)`,
                    r3_catering_hint:       `$ for catering, printed materials and on-site supplies across both cohorts. Too little dents the experience; this stacks on top of any room spend against the $40k cap.`,

                    r4_comms_label:         `Tuesday afternoon: the MeridianHealth CHRO emails "Is Day 1 fully handled?" How do you communicate?`,
                    com_proactive:          `Proactive — a clean, confident note to both clients 24 hours early, before being asked`,
                    com_reactive:           `Reactive — answer the CHRO's email now; tell Cohort B if they raise it`,
                    com_silent:             `Stay quiet — the plan works; no need to worry the clients with the back-of-house churn`,
                    r4_backfill_label:      `A junior coordinator just called in sick. How do you absorb the loss?`,
                    bac_bench:              `Activate the bench — a pre-named backup steps in; no overtime, no budget breach`,
                    bac_stretch:            `Stretch the remaining coordinators across both cohorts — overtime on already-tired staff`,
                    bac_none:               `Run a coordinator short — the plan will hold for one week`,
                    r4_routine_label:       `What issue-tracking and escalation routine governs the week?`,
                    rou_named:              `A named single decision-maker plus a simple issue log — who watches what, who decides`,
                    rou_everyone:           `Everyone watches everything and the team decides together as things come up`,
                    rou_none:               `No formal routine — the plan is solid; deal with surprises if they happen`,
                    r4_confidence_label:    `How firmly do you stand behind the plan as robust, not merely hopeful, when the CHRO probes? (%)`,

                    warn_awareness_range:   `Constraint awareness must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_catering_negative: `Catering & materials spend cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_confidence_range:  `Confidence must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_binding_right: `<strong>That's the binding constraint.</strong> Room 300 is your only hybrid room that seats 50, and it's gone for 9 days — everything else (faculty windows, the suite, the staffing) bends around it. Name the bottleneck first and the rest of the week organizes itself.`,
                    coach_r1_faculty:       `<strong>The faculty windows matter — but they're not the bottleneck.</strong> You can re-time teachers; you cannot conjure a hybrid room that seats 50. Sequence around the dead room first, then fit the faculty into it.`,
                    coach_r1_budget:        `<strong>Leading with the budget puts the cart before the horse.</strong> Minimizing spend is a constraint, not the binding one. If you optimize dollars while the only hybrid room is dark, you'll under-serve Cohort A's contractual requirement and lose the renewal anyway.`,
                    coach_r1_staff:         `<strong>Staffing is tight, but it isn't the bottleneck.</strong> Coordinators can be re-assigned; the hybrid room cannot be replicated on campus. Anchor the plan on the room, then staff to it.`,
                    coach_r1_loudest:       `<strong>That's whack-a-mole.</strong> Grab the first room for Cohort A and you may strand its Wednesday-morning faculty, double-book a coordinator, or bump Cohort B — three new breaks for one fix. Map the cascade before you commit a single move.`,
                    coach_r1_parallel:      `<strong>Parallel fixes without a shared map collide.</strong> Five people each solving their own piece is how the room, the faculty and the staffing decisions contradict each other by Tuesday. Trace the dependencies together first.`,
                    coach_r1_map_sharp:     `<strong>Sharp.</strong> You named the dead hybrid room as the binding constraint AND committed to tracing the full cascade before moving — that's the discipline that stops one fix from spawning three new breaks.`,
                    coach_r1_lowaware:      `<strong>You're about to re-sequence half-blind.</strong> Cohort A's lead faculty is now Wednesday-morning only and Cohort B's guest is fixed Thursday — miss those and you'll build a 'feasible' schedule that's already broken. Register every window first.`,
                    coach_r2_suite_blind:   `<strong>You rented the suite before you'd mapped why.</strong> $16,800 is 42% of your quarterly budget — you've protected one week and starved the next eight programs. Was a split-room option worth a tenth of the cost? When is $16,800 actually worth it?`,
                    coach_r2_suite_ok:      `<strong>The suite gives Cohort A a true hybrid room</strong> — and you went in with eyes open on the 42% it costs. That's a defensible call for the audition cohort, provided the rest of the budget still covers both cohorts' catering and materials.`,
                    coach_r2_split:         `<strong>Split rooms protect the experience for a fraction of the suite's cost.</strong> You'll need a second coordinator to run two spaces, but you've kept Cohort A hybrid without consuming 42% of the budget. Smart portfolio thinking.`,
                    coach_r2_virtual:       `<strong>Virtual is the cheap option, but it changes the program.</strong> 48 clinical managers expecting a marquee in-person Day 1 will feel the drop, and the catering you planned disappears. Use it sparingly, not as the headline format for the audition cohort.`,
                    coach_r2_keep300:       `<strong>Room 300 is under water for 9 days.</strong> 'Hope the dry-out finishes early' is not a plan — it's how Cohort A's Day 1 collapses on camera in front of the CHRO. You need a real room, today.`,
                    coach_r2_wedam:         `<strong>Good — you matched the faculty's only window.</strong> Flipping Cohort A to a Wednesday-morning start keeps the lead faculty and the learning sequence intact. That's solving the room AND the teacher, not one at the other's expense.`,
                    coach_r2_wedpm:         `<strong>You solved the room and broke the teacher.</strong> Cohort A's lead faculty told you this morning she's only free Wednesday morning, yet you've scheduled her for the afternoon. Did you map the dependencies before you moved, or fix the loudest problem first?`,
                    coach_r2_thu:           `<strong>Sliding Day 1 to Thursday buys a day and breaks the sequence.</strong> The opening day sets the tone for the audition cohort, and Thursday collides with Cohort B's fixed guest session. Re-time within the faculty's real window instead.`,
                    coach_r2_downgrade:     `<strong>Downgrading Cohort B's room chips at a promise.</strong> Thirty international executives were told they'd have Room 300 for a marquee session, and their reviews go to a different client. A smaller room is a visible demotion — protect it if you can.`,
                    coach_r2_bump:          `<strong>You bumped Cohort B to save Cohort A.</strong> Cohort A's renewal may be safe, but 30 international executives were promised that room — you've optimized one program and damaged the portfolio. How do you hold ≥ 8.5 on <em>both</em>?`,
                    coach_r3_protect:       `<strong>Protecting Maria is the mature call.</strong> One cohort, a junior plus a named backup elsewhere — you've treated staff capacity as a hard constraint, not an elastic one. A rested coordinator is how Day 1 feels calm instead of chaotic.`,
                    coach_r3_onecohort:     `<strong>Giving Maria a single cohort is reasonable</strong> — she carries Cohort A and a junior supports her. Just make sure Cohort B still has credible senior cover, or you've quietly tilted toward one program.`,
                    coach_r3_both:          `<strong>You gave Maria both flagship cohorts.</strong> She's already at overtime — that's 1.5× on the budget, and by Thursday she's running on no sleep on your highest-stakes client. A tired coordinator is how Day 1 feels chaotic. What's your plan when she burns out mid-week?`,
                    coach_r3_portfolio:     `<strong>That's portfolio staffing.</strong> Senior cover on both cohorts with juniors backstopped means neither program is left exposed — you're protecting the ≥ 8.5 across the portfolio rather than winning one and losing the other.`,
                    coach_r3_aheavy:        `<strong>You loaded Cohort A and left Cohort B exposed.</strong> Two seniors and the good room on A, a lone junior in a half-size space on B. A's renewal is safer — but you've robbed Peter to pay Paul, and Cohort B's reviews go to a client who will notice.`,
                    coach_r3_thin:          `<strong>Spreading one senior thin to save hours backfires.</strong> A single senior stretched across two flagship cohorts plus inexperienced juniors means both programs feel under-supported. You saved hours and spent experience on both.`,
                    coach_r3_lowcatering:   `<strong>You've under-funded catering and materials.</strong> On a flagship cohort, thin coffee and missing printed materials are exactly the small details executives remember. The experience score lives in those details — don't starve them.`,
                    coach_r4_proactive:     `<strong>A clean, confident note 24 hours early is the difference</strong> between 'they had a problem and managed it' and 'they're winging it.' You told both clients before being asked — that's how competent recovery reads as control, not chaos.`,
                    coach_r4_reactive:      `<strong>Answering the email is fine, but it's behind the curve.</strong> The CHRO had to ask. Proactive beats reactive: a confident note before the question lands buys you trust you can't get once the client is already wondering.`,
                    coach_r4_silent:        `<strong>Silence reads as chaos.</strong> You've quietly re-arranged everything and the CHRO has heard nothing — so a competent recovery looks like winging it. A 24-hour-early note was free and would have held the relationship.`,
                    coach_r4_stretch:       `<strong>Stretching tired staff to cover the sick junior buys overtime, not stability.</strong> You're adding hours to people already near the limit on your highest-stakes week. That's how the second surprise becomes a service failure.`,
                    coach_r4_nobackfill:    `<strong>Running a coordinator short is a fragile bet.</strong> One junior is out and you have no bench — the next small thing now has nothing to absorb it. Operations is the business of the second surprise; where's your backfill?`,
                    coach_r4_named:         `<strong>That's a robust, not hopeful, plan.</strong> Proactive client comms, a real bench for the sick coordinator, and a single named decision-maker with an issue log — you've designed for the second surprise instead of praying it doesn't come.`,
                    coach_r4_everyone:      `<strong>'Everyone watches everything' means no one owns anything.</strong> When the next issue lands at 6 a.m., who decides? Shared vigilance without a single named decision-maker turns a fast call into a committee. Name the owner.`,
                    coach_r4_noroutine:     `<strong>Your plan works — if nothing else goes wrong.</strong> No issue log, no escalation rule, no named decision-maker. Then the second surprise lands and there's no one assigned to catch it and no rule for who decides. That's a fragile week.`,
                    coach_overbudget:       `<strong>You've blown past the $40,000 cap.</strong> Every dollar over starves the next eight programs of their materials and catering money. The discipline is portfolio-level: protect this week <em>without</em> mortgaging the quarter.`,

