                    insight_held:           `Both cohorts held: you protected ≥ 8.5 on Cohort A AND Cohort B, on budget, with resilience to spare. You sequenced around the binding room constraint, staffed the portfolio, and recovered proactively — the audition renewal and the international relationship both survived.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `You protected the experience on both cohorts — but you blew the $40,000 cap to do it. A win this week that mortgages the next eight programs isn't an operations win; it's a deferred problem. Was the suite worth 42% when split rooms cost a tenth?`,
                    insight_target_fragile: `You hit ≥ 8.5 on both, but resilience is thin — the plan looks more lucky than robust. Without a real bench and a named decision-maker, the next surprise could still have sunk the week.`,
                    insight_missed:         `You fell short of ≥ 8.5 on at least one cohort. In a service business the experience score is the single strongest predictor of renewal — a miss here is a revenue decision one step removed.`,
                    insight_robbed:         `You saved Cohort A and lost Cohort B — the classic 'rob Peter to pay Paul.' Cohort A's $3.2M renewal may be safe, but 30 international executives got the smaller room and a junior coordinator, and their reviews go to a different client. The portfolio, not one program, was the job.`,
                    insight_blind:          `You committed $16,800 to the hotel suite — 42% of the quarter — before mapping whether a split-room or virtual option could have protected the experience for a fraction of the cost. Budget is a portfolio, not a single week's slush fund.`,
                    insight_overtime:       `You leaned on overtime — assigning the at-risk senior to both cohorts or stretching tired staff. Staff capacity is a hard constraint, not an elastic one; fatigue is how a competent plan still produces a chaotic Day 1.`,
                    insight_silent:         `You recovered quietly and told the clients nothing. Even a flawless back-of-house fix reads as chaos to a client who hears silence. A confident note 24 hours early was free and would have protected the relationship.`,
                    insight_fragile:        `Operational resilience finished low — this was a plan with no bench and no clear escalation ownership. It held this week, but operations is the business of the second surprise, and this plan had nothing in reserve for it.`,
                    insight_robust:         `This is a plan built for the second surprise: a real backfill bench, a single named decision-maker, and proactive client communication. When the next thing breaks — and it always does — you have somewhere to absorb it.`,

                    hint_overbudget:        `You're over the $40,000 cap — every dollar over robs the next eight programs. Reach for split rooms or virtual over the hotel suite, and trim catering to the level that protects the experience without exceeding the quarter.`,
                    hint_portfolio:         `One cohort is far ahead of the other — you're optimizing a single program instead of the portfolio. Re-balance senior coordinators and the good room so both cohorts can clear ≥ 8.5.`,
                    hint_overtime:          `You've triggered overtime on already-tired staff. Capacity is a hard constraint — protect the at-risk senior coordinator and activate a bench instead of stretching people thin.`,
                    hint_resilience:        `Resilience is dangerously low — your plan has no bench and no clear decision-maker. Name a single owner and a backfill before the second surprise lands, or one sick call sinks the week.`,
                    hint_behind:            `One or both cohorts are below a comfortable experience level with little time left. Re-check the binding room constraint, the faculty window, and where your senior coordinators are deployed.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — both cohorts near target and resilience healthy. Lock in proactive client comms and a named decision-maker so the recovery survives the next surprise.`,

